WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.039
Hi, happy Thanksgiving, Ziad.

00:00:02.039 --> 00:00:03.937
Hope you slept in a little.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:08.366
It's Thursday, November twenty-sixth, and the office is closed.

00:00:08.366 --> 00:00:12.935
Big storm day, with one long plan on the calendar this afternoon.

00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:15.818
Here's one to think about while you move.

00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:21.090
About how much more energy does water need to warm up than dry ground does?

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.497
Answer's at the end.

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:25.098
Okay, weather actually matters today.

00:00:25.098 --> 00:00:37.682
Light showers this morning, then steady rain from around one until about nine tonight, maybe one to three inches, with gusts in the forties and fifty-plus possible this afternoon.

00:00:37.682 --> 00:00:45.274
Not a bike day at all, and the wind hangs on into Friday morning before the sun comes back in the afternoon.

00:00:45.274 --> 00:00:46.258
Okay, the day.

00:00:46.258 --> 00:00:50.125
Calendar has a brisk run at seven, thirty-five minutes.

00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:54.906
The morning's your dry-ish window, so that's kind of perfect timing.

00:00:54.906 --> 00:01:01.092
Then nothing until three, when there's a Friendsgiving on there, running till about ten.

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:06.857
That lands right in the worst of the rain, so the train or a car is the easy call.

00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:09.388
And maybe bring a dry pair of socks.

00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:10.372
Alright, news.

00:01:10.372 --> 00:01:11.426
Just two today.

00:01:11.426 --> 00:01:12.481
Closer to home.

00:01:12.481 --> 00:01:18.175
Governor Hochul signed a five-year ban on making or selling toys that run on A.I.

00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:19.652
chatbots in New York.

00:01:19.652 --> 00:01:26.893
So, the talking teddy bear that chats back using the same kind of model as ChatGPT, that's out for now.

00:01:26.893 --> 00:01:37.227
It's one of the first rules like this anywhere, and it's mostly about not knowing yet what these things say to little kids when nobody's listening.

00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:39.336
And in the Gulf, it got worse.

00:01:39.336 --> 00:01:46.929
Yemen's Houthis hit Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura refinery, one of the biggest in the world, and it caught fire.

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:51.850
Another strike hit Riyadh's financial district and hurt eleven people.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.365
The Saudi-led coalition hit back at a Houthi port.

00:01:55.365 --> 00:02:03.450
On top of the tanker mines near Hormuz from a couple of weeks ago, it's a rough stretch for the world's oil routes.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.840
Okay, here's one for next weekend.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:16.455
Sunday, December sixth, Greenpointers is doing its holiday market at Greenpoint Loft, up on the fifth floor of a building on West Street, by the water.

00:02:16.455 --> 00:02:19.900
One to seven, free to get in, five-dollar drinks.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:26.157
Apparently more than sixty local makers, so ceramics, jewelry, prints, vintage, and food.

00:02:26.157 --> 00:02:34.242
There's mulled wine, and someone's doing Polaroid portraits, which you might enjoy just to watch how they light it.

00:02:34.242 --> 00:02:38.179
It's indoors, so whatever the weather does, you're fine.

00:02:38.179 --> 00:02:40.499
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:40.499 --> 00:02:45.631
Since you'll be stuck inside for a bit, shoot the storm through a window.

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:46.896
Make two pictures.

00:02:46.896 --> 00:02:52.590
One focused on the raindrops on the glass, with the street gone soft behind them.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.598
Then one focused on the street, with the drops gone soft.

00:02:56.598 --> 00:03:00.324
Same window, same minute, totally different pictures.

00:03:00.324 --> 00:03:02.925
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:05.877
Remember looking for rain gear for biking?

00:03:05.877 --> 00:03:10.447
Here's how a jacket can keep rain out and still let sweat escape.

00:03:10.447 --> 00:03:15.017
The classic trick is a thin membrane with billions of tiny pores.

00:03:15.017 --> 00:03:22.750
Each pore is something like twenty thousand times smaller than a raindrop, so liquid water can't push through.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.601
But it's hundreds of times bigger than a single water vapor molecule.

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:31.748
So sweat, as vapor, can slip out, while rain stays outside.

00:03:31.748 --> 00:03:33.647
Gore-Tex is the famous one.

00:03:33.647 --> 00:03:36.670
There's a second kind with no holes at all.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:45.809
It's a solid film that kind of soaks up water molecules on the warm side and passes them through, one at a time, to the cool side.

00:03:45.809 --> 00:03:47.848
Slower to start, but tougher.

00:03:47.848 --> 00:03:49.816
Here's the part people miss.

00:03:49.816 --> 00:03:51.433
Breathing isn't a pump.

00:03:51.433 --> 00:03:55.159
Vapor only moves from warm and humid to cool and dry.

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:57.760
So on a cold dry day, it works great.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:05.564
On a warm muggy rainy day, there's barely any difference between inside and out, and it hardly breathes at all.

00:04:05.564 --> 00:04:09.360
That's why you can feel soaked in a waterproof jacket.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.031
It's often your own sweat, not a leak.

00:04:12.031 --> 00:04:14.351
Then there's the outside coating.

00:04:14.351 --> 00:04:19.765
The face fabric gets a water-repellent finish so rain beads up and rolls off.

00:04:19.765 --> 00:04:25.037
When that wears off, the fabric soaks through, which is called wetting out.

00:04:25.037 --> 00:04:32.419
The membrane's still waterproof, but now there's a cold wet layer over it, and breathing basically stops.

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:37.832
A wash and a little heat, like a dryer on low, often brings the beading back.

00:04:37.832 --> 00:04:42.823
And on a bike, you're making way more sweat than any membrane can move.

00:04:42.823 --> 00:04:46.831
So vents under the arms do more than the fanciest fabric.

00:04:46.831 --> 00:04:52.736
So: calendar says a run at seven, then Friendsgiving from three, right in the storm.

00:04:52.736 --> 00:04:54.494
Shoot the rain both ways.

00:04:54.494 --> 00:05:00.610
The answer: about four times as much, which is why the ocean keeps winters here softer.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.367
Happy Thanksgiving, Ziad.

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:03.000
Stay dry.
